WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.262
A TypeScript application may present a
carefully controlled interface,

2
00:00:04.342 --> 00:00:06.770
but the interface is not the security
boundary.

3
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.551
Browsers, mobile applications, desktop
clients, JavaScript bundles,

4
00:00:10.631 --> 00:00:13.605
and API consumers all operate outside the
trusted server.

5
00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:17.387
A user can inspect scripts, modify local
storage, change hidden fields,

6
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:21.350
re-enable disabled buttons, construct
requests with a command-line tool,

7
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.222
or call an endpoint without loading the
intended page.

8
00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:28.185
Request headers and JSON properties are
claims about what the client wants;

9
00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:31.300
they are not proof that the operation is
valid or permitted.

10
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.353
Client-side TypeScript does not change
this trust model.

11
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:38.256
Its types improve editor feedback and
catch mistakes during development,

12
00:00:38.336 --> 00:00:41.006
yet they do not make data sent over HTTP
trustworthy.

13
00:00:41.086 --> 00:00:45.334
Browser validation is still valuable
because it gives prompt feedback and reduces

14
00:00:45.414 --> 00:00:46.749
accidental bad requests.

15
00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.409
Route guards and hidden controls can also
make the interface clearer.

16
00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:51.946
Their purpose is usability.

17
00:00:52.026 --> 00:00:56.031
A security decision must survive when
every client-side control is removed or

18
00:00:56.111 --> 00:00:56.718
rewritten.

19
00:00:56.798 --> 00:01:00.075
The durable rule is simple: the client may
propose an operation,

20
00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:02.461
and the server decides whether to perform
it.

21
00:01:02.541 --> 00:01:05.150
A purchase request can name a product and
quantity,

22
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.839
but the server decides the price and
availability.

23
00:01:07.919 --> 00:01:10.104
A document request can name an identifier,

24
00:01:10.184 --> 00:01:14.493
but the server decides whether the
authenticated subject may access that document.

25
00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.757
An approval request can express an action,

26
00:01:16.837 --> 00:01:20.417
but the server decides whether the current
workflow state permits it.

27
00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:22.985
Good architecture makes this division
visible.

28
00:01:23.065 --> 00:01:26.282
Externally controlled data enters through
a narrow boundary,

29
00:01:26.362 --> 00:01:30.063
trusted identity is supplied by verified
server-side authentication,

30
00:01:30.143 --> 00:01:34.452
and application services apply
authorization and business rules before protected

31
00:01:34.532 --> 00:01:35.321
state changes.

32
00:01:35.401 --> 00:01:39.649
Repositories and database constraints
reinforce the same decisions where shared

33
00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:41.064
invariants require them.

34
00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:45.452
The practical test is whether the server
remains correct when the request comes from

35
00:01:45.532 --> 00:01:49.780
a completely custom client that follows
none of the user interface's assumptions.
